.TH "NIAC" "1" "Nov 2026" "NIAC" "NIAC Manual"

.SH NAME
niac-scenario-generate - Generate a scenario pack


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBniac scenario generate  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Generate the YAML for one scenario pack (see 'niac scenario list').

.PP
With no flags the YAML goes to stdout. -o writes it to a file. --save stores it
in the running daemon's library instead and prints the command that starts it;
the daemon must approve the host interface with --attachment-policy before that
start will pass preflight.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--api\fP=""
	Daemon API address (default: https://127.0.0.1:8445, or NIAC_API_URL)

.PP
\fB--cacert\fP=""
	Daemon certificate to trust (default: the local daemon's own, when visible)

.PP
\fB--force\fP[=false]
	With --save, replace a library network of the same name

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for generate

.PP
\fB--insecure\fP[=false]
	Skip TLS verification, for a daemon whose certificate this host cannot see

.PP
\fB-o\fP, \fB--output\fP=""
	Write the YAML to this file instead of stdout

.PP
\fB--save\fP=""
	Save into the running daemon's library under this name


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--storage-keep\fP=500
	Run history records to keep, pruned oldest first on start (0 keeps every run)

.PP
\fB--storage-path\fP=""
	Path to NIAC run history database (default: ~/.niac/niac.db)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # Write the hospital pack to a file
  niac scenario generate hospital -o hospital.yaml

  # Store it in the running daemon's library, then start it
  niac scenario generate hospital --save hospital
  niac simulation start -i en0 --config hospital.yaml --attachment cyberscope --attachment-mode direct
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBniac-scenario(1)\fP


